  "tutWiresTitle": "How the abacus works",
  "tutWiresBody": "Each wire is a place. The bottom one is the ones, above it the tens, then the hundreds, and so on.\n\nThere are 10 beads on a wire. The beads we count are pushed to the left; the free ones stay on the right. The two black beads in the middle are the fifth and the sixth: they show 5 at a glance, no need to count again.",
  "tutSetTitle": "How to set a number",
  "tutSetBody": "A number is set from its biggest place, left to right. 254 is 2 hundreds, 5 tens and 4 ones: push in 2 beads on the hundreds wire, 5 on the tens wire and 4 on the ones wire.\n\nA 0 in the number means: leave that wire alone.",
  "tutAddTitle": "Rule 1. Adding",
  "tutAddBody": "We add from the biggest place too. A wire holds at most 9 beads. If there are still no more than 9 after adding, there is room: just push in as many beads as the digit says.\n\n23 + 45: push in 4 on the tens, 5 on the ones. That makes 68.",
  "tutFriendsTitle": "Pairs that make 10",
  "tutFriendsBody": "Remember the pairs that together make 10:\n1 and 9,  2 and 8,  3 and 7,  4 and 6,  5 and 5.\n\nThe abacus shows it at once: push in 7 beads and 3 are left on the right. We need these pairs to carry and to borrow.",
  "tutCarryTitle": "Rule 2. Carrying a ten",
  "tutCarryBody": "10 beads on one wire are 1 bead on the wire above. If there is no room, push in 1 bead on the wire above and push back the digit's partner to 10 here:\n+ 7 = + 10 − 3.\n\n5 + 7: the ones already have 5, so 7 won't fit. Push in 1 ten and push back 3 on the ones. That makes 12.",
  "tutSubTitle": "Rule 3. Taking away",
  "tutSubBody": "We take away from the biggest place too. If there are enough beads pushed in, push back as many as the digit says.\n\n58 − 26: push back 2 on the tens and 6 on the ones. 32 are left.",
  "tutBorrowTitle": "Rule 4. Borrowing a ten",
  "tutBorrowBody": "If fewer beads are pushed in than you need to take away, borrow a ten: push back 1 bead on the wire above and push in the digit's partner to 10 here:\n− 7 = − 10 + 3.\n\n12 − 7: the ones have only 2. Push back 1 ten and push in 3 on the ones. 5 are left.",
  "tutChainTitle": "Past 9 and past 0",
  "tutChainBody": "Sometimes the wire above has no room either: it already has 9. Then rule 2 works for it too: + 1 = + 10 − 9. The one goes further up, and 9 are pushed back here.\n\n99 + 1: hundreds + 1, tens − 9, ones − 9. That makes 100.\nTaking away works the same way, past 0: 100 − 1 = 99.",
  "tutTryTitle": "Your turn",
  "tutTryBody": "Tap the calculator and type your own example: the abacus will solve it and explain every step below by these rules.\n\nIn Exercises you solve examples yourself.",
  "menuTilt": "Tilt moves the beads",
  "menuSound": "Sound"
